heart-tts (0.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Ship the audio.cpp build with the NEON sanoTTS decoder as audiocpp_cli and enable
    it (sanotts.cpu_decoder=neon). Full "heart" voice: ~1.5x faster per sentence
    (376 -> 249 ms measured), same audio (max 1 LSB difference). HEART_NEON=off
    returns to the old decoder.
  * New command heart-tts-bench: audio length made vs processing time (per-process, kept-loaded,
    and full path incl. leveler), x-real-time.
  * Faster time-to-first-sound: numpy is imported lazily (~270 ms saved at start), gain/gate/
    consonant finish is native (hn-fast --finish for heart-nano, bundled heart-finish for heart),
    and the ffmpeg leveler is ONE long-lived process piped into pw-play instead of one ffmpeg per
    chunk. Falls back to the old per-chunk path if streaming fails. HEART_STREAM=off,
    HEART_NATIVE_FINISH=off restore the old behaviour.
  * Kept-loaded engines: heart-nano via "hn-fast --serve" (exits after 30 min idle),
    heart via a bundled audiocpp_server started on demand (idle watchdog, port 8098).
    Skips ~65-100 ms of process start / model load per sentence. HEART_SERVE=off
    returns to one process per sentence; any server failure falls back automatically.
  * Engine switch: auto / gpu / cpu-neon. Set HEART_ENGINE=..., the "engine" menu in the Read Aloud
    window (remembered in ~/.config/heart-tts/engine), or "heart-say --engine gpu ...".
    cpu-neon = the CPU's ARM NEON vector code (every Pi); gpu = heart-nano's decoder on the Pi
    graphics chip (V3D; needs less CPU, not faster; heart-nano only, falls back to cpu-neon if the GPU
    files, driver or access are missing); auto = whatever is fastest here (today cpu-neon).
    Bundles the GPU files in /opt/heart-tts/gpu (libhngpu.so, shaders, plan); uses the system Vulkan
    driver. heart-tts-doctor reports which engines are available and why not. The full "heart" voice
    has no GPU engine. (An int8 dot-product engine was tested and rejected: slower, slightly different
    sound. hn-fast still accepts --engine cpu-dotprod, experimental, not selectable here.)

 -- Tarel <334372633+tarelerulz@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:40:00 +0000

heart-tts (0.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Faster heart-nano: bundle the hand-written "hn-fast" CPU engine and auto-select
    it for the heart-nano voice (~2x faster than the audio.cpp path, byte-identical
    audio). The "heart" voice still uses the audio.cpp engine.
  * read_heart.py now picks the fastest available engine per voice and hardware: a
    hn-fast-gpu (V3D GPU) binary if present, else hn-fast (CPU), else audio.cpp.
    No GPU binary is shipped (it needs a custom V3D Vulkan driver), but the hook is
    in place if one is dropped in.
  * heart-tts-doctor reports which engine each voice will use.

 -- Tarel <334372633+tarelerulz@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 02:30:00 +0000

heart-tts (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release. "Read Aloud" text-to-speech for the Raspberry Pi, packaged
    from the native audio.cpp sanoTTS engine:
    - read-aloud: a floating Wayland window; highlight text anywhere, click READ.
    - heart-say: speak text (or the clipboard) aloud from the terminal.
    - heart-tts-doctor: health check + synthesis self-test.
  * Bundles the "heart" and "heart-nano" voice models (GPL-3, ampixa/sanoTTS).
    The audio.cpp engine is Apache-2.0 (ShugoAI LLC).
  * Playback through PipeWire (pw-play) so it follows the current output device
    and mixes with other audio, with an ALSA fallback.

